  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong low pressure system will bring widespread\nrainfall, and localized flooding to the area on Thanksgiving \nDay.\n\nA complex upper level pattern will lead to unsettled weather \nthrough the Thanksgiving holiday across the region. Two upper \nlevel shortwave features, with one across the Gulf states, and \nanother across the central U.S. will phase, which is currently\nresulting in the rapid development of a deepening low pressure \nsystem that will move up the east coast through Friday.\n\nWith rainfall at the areas doorstep this afternoon, conditions\nwill start to go downhill across the area from south to north as\nthe rain shield enters and pressure gradient tightens across the\narea. REFS probabilities of greater than 2.0 inches are in the\n80-100% range for all of SW Virginia and NW North Carolina zones\nin the forecast area. Most of SE West Virginia will likely see\nsome semblance of a rain shadow effect; however, rainfall totals\nhave increased across these areas, with probabilities of seeing\ngreater than 2.0 inches in the 30-40% range. Current expected\nrainfall totals of 2.0 to 3.0 inches of rainfall is expected for\nareas along and east of the Blue Ridge, with localized totals up\nto 5 inches possible right along the Blue Ridge itself due to\nthe intense LLJ enhanced upslope forecast to occur across these\nareas. For areas west of the Blue Ridge, 1.0-2.0 inches of\nrainfall is expected. Much of this rainfall will be beneficial\nfor the area given the ongoing drought conditions; however,\nlocalized areas along and east of the Blue Ridge may have some\nflooding issues due to freshly fallen leaves that may pose an \nurban flooding threat due to clogged drains and culverts during \nthe heaviest rainfall period early Thursday morning. Given this\npotential, a Flood Watch has been issued for these areas where\nthese issues may arise. \n\nAs the low continues to deepen and move north into New England\non Friday, wraparound showers and snow showers will be likely\nfor the favored western slopes. Light snowfall accumulations\nlook possible in western Greenbrier and locations south into NW\nNorth Carolina Friday night.\n\nCalmer weather returns for the weekend with temperatures warming\nback to above normal through early next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong winds will accompany the passage of the \nlow pressure system.\n\nA secondary threat will be very gusty winds across the region \nas a result of the rapidly deepening low. A significant low \nlevel jet nearing 80kts will move overhead tomorrow, which will\ntranslate to gusty east/northeast surface winds beginning late \ntonight and especially tomorrow morning. Wind gusts exceeding \n35 to 40mph will be common with occasional higher gusts nearing \nthe 50-65 mph range along the higher ridges of the Blue Ridge \nand also portions central Virginia. Probabilities of Wind gusts\nreaching Wind Advisory criteria are now greater than 75% \nareawide. This area of concern for an upgrade would be from \nCharlotte County to Amherst County where a favorable barrier jet\ncould produce gusts into the 50mph range. REFS wind gusts \nprobabilities for exceeding 50mph gusts in the aforementioned \nlocations are approaching 50%...leading to higher confidence of \nthis corridor receiving a period of higher magnitude gusts. A\nHigh Wind Warning has also been issued across portions of\nCentral Virginia, where the LLJ is expected to lead to\nparticularly intense wind gusts up to 65 mph along ridge lines \nand up to 45-50mph in valley locations. These areas had the \nhighest probabilities in the range of 40-80% from the REFS in \nseeing greater than 55mph wind gusts. Significant downsloping \neasterly winds in southeast West Virginia will also occur, with \na High Wind Warning maintained for Western Greenbrier.",
